WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
Current questions feel much lighter once the learner hears an ampere as a rate sentence.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.360
One ampere means one coulomb passes a point each second.

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.040
That immediately tells you why multiplying current by time gives total charge, and why dividing charge by time gives current.

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.720
The biggest trap is letting total charge and current collapse into one idea.

5
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.400
This lesson works best when the checkpoint-rate story stays visible in every calculation.

6
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.080
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 2 becomes calmer once current is treated as a rate and charge as the amount accumulated over time.

7
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.760
Current means charge each second.

8
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.440
An ampere is a rate of charge flow, not a stored amount.

9
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.120
Charge means total amount passed.

10
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.800
Charge tells you how much passed the checkpoint over the full time interval.

11
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.204
This diagram matters because it keeps the checkpoint idea visible.

12
00:01:03.204 --> 00:01:09.608
Current is not the total number of carriers in the whole loop.

13
00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:16.011
It is how much charge passes one chosen point per second.

14
00:01:16.011 --> 00:01:22.415
If the same total charge passes in less time, the current is larger because the rate is greater.

15
00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:28.819
Once the learner can say that sentence out loud, the move between Q equals I t and I equals Q over t becomes much easier.

16
00:01:28.819 --> 00:01:35.222
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong current answer keeps the checkpoint, the time, and the total passed clearly separate.

17
00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:41.626
Checkpoint: Use one chosen point in the loop to define the current measurement.

18
00:01:41.626 --> 00:01:48.030
Timer: The time interval decides how long the rate has been running.

19
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.466
A turnstile analogy helps because it makes rate and amount feel different immediately.

20
00:01:53.466 --> 00:01:58.902
The crowd-per-second at the gate is the current story.

21
00:01:58.902 --> 00:02:04.338
The total number of people who passed during the full event is the charge story.

22
00:02:04.338 --> 00:02:09.774
If the same crowd total passes in a shorter time, the gate rate must be larger.

23
00:02:09.774 --> 00:02:15.210
That is exactly the logic the learner needs when comparing loops that move the same charge in different times.

24
00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:20.646
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy helps the learner defend why current is a rate even when the same total charge is involved.

25
00:02:20.646 --> 00:02:26.082
Per-second count means current idea.

26
00:02:26.082 --> 00:02:31.518
The gate rate matches charge passing a checkpoint each second.

27
00:02:31.518 --> 00:02:36.954
Event total means charge idea.

28
00:02:36.954 --> 00:02:42.390
The full count over the whole interval matches the total charge passed.

29
00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:48.363
If current is charge per second, then multiplying that rate by the number of seconds gives total charge.

30
00:02:48.363 --> 00:02:54.336
That is all Q equals I t is saying.

31
00:02:54.336 --> 00:03:00.309
Reversing the same idea gives I equals Q over t.

32
00:03:00.309 --> 00:03:06.282
The only common danger is forgetting that the time must be in seconds when the current is in amperes and the charge is in coulombs.

33
00:03:06.282 --> 00:03:12.255
Once that unit discipline is secure, the calculations become very straightforward.

34
00:03:12.255 --> 00:03:18.228
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works once the learner can read Q = I t as a direct translation of the rate story.

35
00:03:18.228 --> 00:03:24.201
Direction one: Known current and time give total charge.

36
00:03:24.201 --> 00:03:30.174
Direction two: Known charge and time give current.

37
00:03:30.174 --> 00:03:36.147
I = Q / t.

38
00:03:36.147 --> 00:03:42.120
Translate minutes into seconds before substituting into Q = I t or I = Q / t.

39
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.488
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

40
00:03:47.488 --> 00:03:52.856
First say what the current means at the checkpoint in coulombs per second.

41
00:03:52.856 --> 00:03:58.224
Second convert the time into seconds if needed.

42
00:03:58.224 --> 00:04:03.592
Third multiply or divide depending on whether the unknown is the total charge or the current.

43
00:04:03.592 --> 00:04:08.960
That routine prevents the calculation from floating free of the meaning and prevents most unit mistakes at the same time.

44
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.328
Keep this takeaway in view: Checkpoint first, seconds second, operation third is the safest routine for Lesson 2.

45
00:04:14.328 --> 00:04:19.696
Step 1 means read the rate.

46
00:04:19.696 --> 00:04:25.064
Translate amperes back into charge per second before substituting.

47
00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:30.432
Step 2 means fix the time unit.

48
00:04:30.432 --> 00:04:35.800
Convert minutes into seconds when the relation uses amperes and coulombs.

49
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.430
Run four checks.

50
00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:45.060
One ampere means one coulomb per second.

51
00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.690
Current is a rate, not a stored amount.

52
00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:54.320
Q equals I t when charge is the unknown, and I equals Q over t when current is the unknown.

53
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.950
And the time should be in seconds when amperes and coulombs are being used together.

54
00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.580
Those four checks cover the whole lesson structure.

55
00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.210
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain why the same total charge delivered faster means a larger current.

56
00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:12.840
Ampere means coulomb per second.

57
00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.470
Translate the unit meaning before touching the symbols.

58
00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.100
Charge means rate times time.

59
00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.730
Use Q = I t when the current and the duration are known.

60
00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:33.347
Start by saying what the current means in charge per second.

61
00:05:33.347 --> 00:05:39.964
Then convert the time into seconds if the question gives minutes.

62
00:05:39.964 --> 00:05:46.581
Use Q equals I t when the unknown is the total charge passed, or I equals Q over t when the unknown is the current.

63
00:05:46.581 --> 00:05:53.198
Finish by restating the meaning: a larger current means the same checkpoint sees more charge each second.

64
00:05:53.198 --> 00:05:59.815
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

65
00:05:59.815 --> 00:06:06.432
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 2 improves immediately once the learner treats current as a rate story rather than as a mysterious symbol.

66
00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:13.049
Do not skip the unit meaning: An ampere should be heard as coulombs per second before the numbers are combined.

67
00:06:13.049 --> 00:06:19.666
Do not skip the seconds conversion: Most mistakes in this lesson come from mixing minutes with amperes.

68
00:06:19.666 --> 00:06:26.283
translate amperes into coulombs per second.

69
00:06:26.283 --> 00:06:32.900
The best answers keep the rate meaning alive all the way through the arithmetic.
